*.hex
*.o
*.elf
*.DS_Store
*.d

build/
build_*/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Generated by tools/gen_compile_commands.py - machine-specific absolute
# paths, regenerate with `make -C grbl/platform/<platform> compdb`.
compile_commands.json

README.md

# The repo-wide `README.md` ignore rule above matches README.md in any
# directory; explicitly un-ignore this one so it can be tracked.
!tools/README.md

# Every grbl/platform/<name>/README.md is a real, tracked porting doc
# (samd21, stm32f103, sg2002, ... already are - this just makes the
# existing convention explicit) - including grbl/platform/_template/'s,
# whose whole point is to be copied into every new port's directory. Without
# this, `git add` on a freshly copied _template/ silently drops its README.
!grbl/platform/*/README.md

# SAME TRAP, one more time: artifacts/README.md documents the tracked
# build-artifact tree (PLAN.md "build artifacts tracked in git" batch) and
# is a real, tracked doc like the two exceptions above - without this it
# silently vanishes from `git add -A` exactly like tools/README.md did
# before its own exception landed.
!artifacts/README.md

# SAME CLASS OF TRAP, caught before landing (Phase 6 rolling #4 review):
# grbl/platform/ch570/vendor/ISP572.o is a deliberately vendored, TRACKED
# binary (Apache-2.0, openwch/ch570 - LICENSE copied alongside in the same
# directory) - line 2's blanket `*.o` rule would otherwise silently drop
# it from every `git add -A`, exactly like the README.md rule above did
# for platform docs until that exception landed. This is the ONE
# vendored-binary exception in this tree; see CONTRACTS.md's CH570
# gap-log entry for why the flash program/erase algorithm is carried as a
# linked object instead of reimplemented (the vendor's own datasheet
# states it deliberately does not document the register protocol this
# function uses, and disassembly confirms real, non-trivial logic, not a
# thin trampoline). Verify with `git check-ignore -v` after ANY future
# .gitignore edit - a silent re-swallow here breaks every fresh clone's
# link, not just this session's build.
!grbl/platform/ch570/vendor/ISP572.o

# SAME CLASS OF TRAP again (PLAN.md "build artifacts tracked in git for
# observability" batch, owner directive): artifacts/<port>/*.hex is
# deliberately TRACKED - RELEASE bin/hex + a plain-text symbol-size map
# (.syms) per buildable port, so every port's CURRENT byte-level state is
# diffable over time (git log -p / git diff), not just AVR's golden MD5.
# Line 1's blanket `*.hex` would otherwise silently drop every one of them
# from `git add -A`, exactly like the README.md and ch570-vendor-.o
# exceptions above. .bin/.syms/MANIFEST.sha256 need no exception - no
# blanket rule above matches those extensions. See tools/build_artifacts.py
# and artifacts/README.md for what generates this tree and the refresh
# policy. Verify with `git check-ignore -v` after ANY future .gitignore
# edit - a silent re-swallow here breaks observability for every port at
# once, not just one.
!artifacts/**/*.hex

# artifacts/<port>/*.elf is the ONE exception to the "un-ignore what's
# tracked" pattern above, and DELIBERATELY SO ("Эльф на тегах" owner
# directive, PLAN.md): unlike bin/hex/syms, ELF is tracked ONLY at
# release-tag time, not on every refresh - it is the largest artifact
# class (48-166KB/unit vs. bin 25-95KB, hex 72-116KB, syms a few KB) and
# git cannot delta binaries across recompiles, so tracking it on every
# refresh cost a full ~2.6MB snapshot again each time (measured: one
# refresh grew .git from 12MB to 14MB). So *.elf under artifacts/ is left
# covered by line 3's blanket `*.elf` rule ON PURPOSE - no negation here.
#
# This means the usual "just `git add -A`" tag-time flow does NOT work for
# ELF: `git add -A`/`git add .` silently SKIP ignored paths with zero
# output, which is exactly the failure mode that bit tools/README.md,
# ISP572.o, and (in the old, now-reverted, always-tracked policy)
# artifacts/*.elf|hex THREE times before - a real file quietly missing
# from a commit, discovered later, not now. A fourth blanket-ignore
# surprise is not acceptable, so tag time must NOT rely on `-A` for these
# files: it force-adds them BY EXPLICIT PATH instead -
#   python3 tools/build_artifacts.py build --with-elf
#   git add -f artifacts/*/*.elf
#   git commit -m "artifacts: tag vX.Y.Z ELF snapshot"
# `git add -f` on an already-ignored path either adds it (success) or, if
# the path is wrong/missing, errors LOUDLY ("did not match any files") -
# there is no silent-skip code path when `-f` + an explicit glob is used,
# unlike `-A`. Proof required after ANY future .gitignore edit touching
# this rule: `git check-ignore -v artifacts/<port>/grbl_<port>.elf` must
# report line 3 (`*.elf`) as the matching rule with no committed file
# present (default state), and `git add -f` on that same path must
# succeed once `build --with-elf` has produced it (tag-time state).
